### What wakes you

The world wakes a role for what it owes, never for what is said:

- the objective: at the start for `idea-repo`, which keeps the brief; for `idea-review-repo`, once `idea-repo` writes the brief, delivers or ends its first turn; for `story-repo`, which keeps the outline, once the world then falls quiet; for every other role once `story-repo` writes the outline or the world falls quiet again, except `internal-review-repo`, whose objective waits until a version is offered for approval; nothing wakes a role before its objective does, and what it is asked waits for it;
- a request another role opens or reopens in your repository (an issue, or a pull request), or the asker adding to an open request you have already seen, or asking you again for its review;
- the owner's first reply in your own open request since you opened, reopened or last added to it (its later replies wait for your next turn), or your request closed by another role, to check the answer; another role merging a pull request of yours does not wake you, closing it unmerged does;
- a review asked of you on a pull request, until you review it (on a version's pull request into `release` or `final` of `paper-repo`, only `internal-review-repo`'s review counts, so only it is woken); another role asking for changes on your pull request, or approving your pull request into your own repository once no review asked on it is still owed, unless Forgejo merges it then as you scheduled (a pull request into another role's repository is its owner's to merge);
- an issue another role opens in its own repository and assigns to you, or its owner adding to one you have already seen; unassigning you or closing it settles it;
- the assignee's first reply in an issue you assigned in your own repository, or its closing that issue;
- an issue in your repository that waits on another (its dependency), once that one closes;
- `idea-repo` writing or changing the `Brief` page of its wiki, which you work from;
- the asker adding to its request in your repository after it was closed, unless only answers, replies or additions woke the turn it wrote in;
- a gap in the delivery that is yours to resolve, or a reason the release was rejected;
- a job you hold (`ht-resource run` or `track`) ending, or printing a line that starts with `HUTIAN_WAKE:` (at most once in 30 minutes).

Everything else (other comments, edited or deleted comments, pushes, releases, other wiki pages, assigning an issue to yourself, and labels) is information for your next activation, not a reason to wake you. A running role can read posted Forgejo state at any time within its access; a new request wakes its owner when it is free to take another turn. If only a reply or an addition woke your turn, your comments wake nobody unless you also deliver (push, `ht-role guide delivery`) before it ends; without the push they remain readable, but cause no further wake. Opening a new request wakes its owner even then. Nothing you write reaches anyone until it is posted.

### Turns

`ht-role execution` shows the activation as it stood when the turn started, and, read live, what you owe that was opened, added to, answered or closed since then, by whom and where. Your conversation goes on across turns until one does not end cleanly, its context passes the world's limit, or your worker's configuration changes.

### Requests and answers

A request is an issue you open in the repository of the role that can supply what you need, one issue per need: material your part needs from it, or a fix or a decision in its part. What you produce reaches the roles that use it: deliver it, and do not open requests to hand it on or to say how to use it. Say in a request:

- what you need, as concretely as you can (data, a result, a decision, a figure, a review);
- what it is for: the claim, decision or deliverable it feeds;
- which part of your work waits for it.

